A||||12||Doni Hagan||The death of a Fiero||||9||
Z||000000||Doni Hagan||08-30-2001||01:26 AM||||Today, I watched while a wrecking yard put an 88 T-top carcass in the crusher. For the past couple of days, I've been negotiating with the yard manager to get parts off of it before it was destroyed, some of which I pulled myself. <P>I've seen cars crushed before but never one I had anything to do with. Plus, as badly as I'd like a T-top conversion, the car didn't have enough left to even get a decent start on one. <P>Now it's history. It was kind of sad in a way and made me think about the first owner as well as the last. The car only had about 109K on the speedo. What did it do to end up in a wrecking yard? Did it really piss off the last owner that much, enough for him/her to say "Junk the thing?"<P>I enjoy playing with my GT. I've given up most of the destructive things I used to do. The Fiero affords me a chance to be creative and relax at the same time. <P>Anyway, sorry this is so long... Watching that car get crushed today kinda bummed me out. <P> ||||reg||9
Z||000001||Urchin||08-30-2001||01:59 AM||||That's depressing.  I had a similar experience, though I didn't witness the death itself.  There was a '87 coupe in great shape in the local wreckers here not too long ago.  I must have been first to spot it.  There was absolutely nothing off of it.  I took what ever I could carry at the time (nose, rear bumper, moldings, interior trim, etc.  The car looked like you could have driven it out (minus the wheels).  Fairly low klms, clean engine.  You gotta wonder what people are thinking sometimes.  I came back a few weeks later and it had been striped clean and had been moved to the "death row" isle.   ... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/side_cut_sig_4.jpg">  <BR>If you can read this, I'm parked.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Doni Hagan||08-30-2001||12:19 PM||||The upside of the experience is that the yard owner (who now calls me "Mr. Fiero") is gonna contact me as soon as another one gets there and give me 1st crack at it. He said he gets about 1 or 2 every 4-5 weeks or so. <P>Cool!!||||reg||14
Z||000003||FieroMaster88||08-30-2001||01:07 PM||||DOH!!!  That sucks.  I would have purchased what was left of the car and restored it.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>If they ever get any Fastbacks in...dont car how bad condition, as long as it is not crashed really bad (bent frame) let me know!  I want a fastback too!<P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FieroMaster88.gif">            <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>88 Coupe With a new V6<BR>5 speed Isuzu trans<BR>AcCel 8mm wires<BR>rapid fire plugs<BR>custom intake with K&N filter.<BR>Soon to be 2" closer to the ground and have a turbo||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfiero||08-30-2001||01:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Doni Hagan:<BR><B>The upside of the experience is that the yard owner (who now calls me "Mr. Fiero").... </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Watch it!  I have that name copyrighted......there can be only one Mr. Fiero!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||12
Z||000005||Fformula88||08-30-2001||05:48 PM||||That really is terrible, especially with it being an 88 T-top.  The owner must not have known he could have gotten a few $$ for it, even if it wasn't running.<P>I helped put a Fiero to the crusher too, but it was in the name of saving a Fiero.  After wrecking an 87 coupe lastwinter, I found anouther 87 coupe in a yard which was nearly intact!  The interior wasn't very good on it since it had sat out all winter without a windshield, but I was able to extract nearly every body panel!  I also took the headlight motors, door hinges (the yard guys hated me for that one  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  and a bunch of other small items.  The best part is I now have a huge coffee can full of Fiero bolts, screws and other fasteners!  They have come in really handy.<P>The 87 I wrecked is still in the process of rebuilding, mostly by my uncle now.  Its going to be my cousin's first car!  He's sanding down the frame to get rid of the rust and will be painting it Yellow (to match his 79 Trans Am).  So at least that junked Fiero will live on in another!<P>------------------<BR>John  <BR>AOLIM: Fformula88<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fierosig2.JPG"> <BR>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cheever3000||08-30-2001||06:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>What did it do to end up in a wrecking yard?<HR></BLOCKQUOTE>I'm guessing there was a wreck or some damage that the insurance company decided to total it instead of pay to repair.<P>That's what my insurance company tried to do to me.  They wouldn't approve a $4700 repair bill (bodywork only, mind you) on my 88 GT with 62000 miles and in otherwise perfect condition.  But I insisted on keeping it, of course.  Someone else apparently didn't want to fix theirs.  That's my guess.  Very sad.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR><I>Ranger Chuck</I><BR><A HREF="http://home.houston.rr.com/fieropartsrelay" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Parts Relay</A> administrator||||reg||1
